  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWill be focusing this part of the forecast in the wake of the \nongoing convective activity in the Great Lakes. Low pressure to \nshift to the east of the CWA today with a cold front coming through \nacross Lake Erie by 18Z today. Expect some additional convective \nactivity along the cold front but the possibility for severe at this \npoint should be greatly diminished. Storms that form will be \nfighting dry air aloft and limited instability, but thunder should \nstill be expected in a scattered nature as the cold front tracks \nthrough. From there, the whole stacked low pressure system to the \neast will strengthen, and create a brief period where weak trough \naxes rotating through in the cyclonic flow and cool air over the \nwarm lake will produce lake enhanced showers late this evening into \ntonight. Expecting a small amount of near surface based CAPE capped \nat around 800mb with an inversion at that level. High pressure \nbuilding from the west will shut this potential for showers down \nfairly quickly. \n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure and dry weather with a cooler airmass settles into \nplace for the end of the week. Conditions will begin to become \nunsettled over the weekend with a 500mb trough moving into the Great \nLakes via the upper Mississippi Valley accompanied with an inverted \nsurface trough from surface low pressure over the lower Mississippi \nValley. Will expect convective chances to increase with gradually \nrising dewpoints once again and temperatures back into the low to \nmid 80s.",
